# Binary assets must never be line-ending converted.
#
# Git decides "text or binary" by looking for a NUL byte in the first 8000 bytes. A
# Radiance `.hdr` starts with an ASCII header followed by RGBE pixel data, and a gradient
# sky happens to contain no NUL early on — so git classified the starter HDRIs as TEXT and,
# with `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default), a fresh checkout inserted a 0x0D before
# every 0x0A in the pixel data. Measured, not theorised: 131121 bytes committed, 131125
# bytes checked out.
#
# The failure mode is the worst kind. It cannot happen on the machine that generated the
# file, and it cannot happen in CI (Linux runners default to `autocrlf=false`), so both
# "works on my machine" and "CI is green" hold while every Windows clone gets a corrupt
# environment map. Declaring the types is the only thing that makes it impossible.
# T-223 · the same failure, one file over. The byte-for-byte gate regenerates
# `library/manifest.json` and compares it with the committed copy — and the generator writes
# LF while a Windows checkout produces CRLF, because this path had no declaration here. The
# note above stops at binaries; the gate needed it extended to the one TEXT file the
# generator owns. Measured the same way: 2697 bytes generated, 2783 bytes / 86 CR checked out
# via `git checkout-index`. CI (Linux) would have stayed green forever while every Windows
# clone failed the gate and was told to re-run a generator that changes nothing.
packages/editor/public/library/manifest.json text eol=lf

*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.webp  binary
*.gif   binary
*.hdr   binary
*.exr   binary
*.glb   binary
*.ktx2  binary
*.bin   binary
*.wasm  binary
*.w3p   binary
*.wav   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.ogg   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.webm  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ico   binary
